            {
               "text": "### Prerequisites\n\nThis text continues the initial texts from the first chapter of Volume II of the *First Steps* section (<htcite0>). We therefore suggest studying them before you proceed.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "f1e985f6-ee34-407a-9b47-7ac1a3c8bc48",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "First Steps (Volume 2)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nBy inserting values between the numbers we use to count, we fill the void that existed between them (<htcite0>). This achievement did not discard the numbers we already had; it complemented them.\n\nFilling empty spaces is something we always do, sometimes even without realizing it. With this in mind, let's reflect.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "3bcce27b-3a3a-4a20-b294-1b98f7f72728",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Naming",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: Equal to...</b><br /><br />Can you think of situations where something was missing and you filled the gap?</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_mesoamerica",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### An Indigenous (*Abya Yala*) Example\n\nThe reconstructions made by the Indigenous peoples of Mesoamerica — such as the Maya (<htcite0>) — share features with the numbers we begin to study in this volume. See **Figure 1**.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "daae87a2-5fa9-43d1-88fb-6713b25d50d5",
                     "text": "Tuarez Mejia, L. F.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2023",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgCopanTemple\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgCopanTemple', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 1</b>: Sign at the Copán archaeological site (<htcite0>). Photo taken on <htdate1> during a visit to the site.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "1e007481-9bd3-4a09-ad4d-6e812fe79641",
                     "text": "Instituto Hondureño de Antropologia e Historia",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "02",
                        "day": "01"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2024",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  },
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2015",
                     "month": "7",
                     "day": "30"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Now, let's reflect before continuing.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: I, you, he/she...</b><br /><br />Observe the structure at Copán, Honduras.<br /><br />Where would you place the numbers we use for counting (0, 1, 2...)?<br /><br />Where would you place the fractions (1/2, 1/5, 2/7)?<br /><br />Where would you place the number pi (π) (which is not just 3.14)?<br /><br />Where would you place the other numbers, such as 1, 1.2, 1.3, 2, or 2.4?</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_natural",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Natural Numbers (ℕ)\n\nNatural numbers, identified by ℕ, were the first numbers addressed in *History Tracers* (<htcite0>). With them, we learn to count, add, and multiply in the first five chapters of the *First Steps* section (<htcite1>).\n\n#### Definition\n\nAlthough we use these numbers every day, whether natural numbers include zero or not can depend on the scholar and the historical period being studied (<htcite2>).\n\n#### Visualization\n\nWe can visualize natural numbers through our own body: when we count on our fingers (**Figure 2**), each finger stands for one more unit (<htcite3>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b5efc8f9-c268-430f-9681-7a43848ee48e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "First steps (Volume 1)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b5efc8f9-c268-430f-9681-7a43848ee48e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "First steps (Volume 1)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "ea43d68d-8004-4fa8-bf58-82b57a6458ae",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "eaa59295-fb80-4c33-b1e4-bcdf3c11dabf",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Adding the same numbers",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "5",
                        "day": "9"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table class=\"book_navigation\"><tr><td>Left hand</td><td>Right hand</td></tr><tr><td><img id=\"imgLH\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgLH', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/></td><td><img id=\"imgRH\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgRH', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/></td></tr></table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 2</b>: Representation of the two hands. The source code is available in the project's GitHub repository (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "240800f3-ede4-4deb-9d4e-915a3a03fa68",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Source code",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2024",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "The figure shows a person's two hands raised with all fingers extended.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Besides our fingers, when we study multiplication we learn that we can represent the results of a multiplication with graphs. On such a graph, we mark on the horizontal axis the value being multiplied by the number *N*, move up the vertical axis until we reach the result, and mark the point (<htcite0>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b6428e19-afcf-4d3b-916d-46e5a332ef53",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Understanding the Multiplication Table (0 and 1)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htChart\"><canvas aria-label=\"chart\" id=\"chart3\" style=\"width:30em !important; height:20em !important;\"></canvas></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 1</b>: Representation of the points of the multiplication table for the number one. Graph made using the <htcite0> library.</div></p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "6efe7632-faed-4080-a5aa-38463d43e6d7",
                     "text": "Chart JS",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_rational",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Rational Numbers (ℚ)\n\nRational numbers, represented by ℚ, are numbers that we can write in fraction form, as demonstrated in **Formula 1** (<htcite0>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "bcdd77a7-5c6a-45ed-982e-e370571bca39",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "Rational Numbers",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2010",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math displaystyle=\"false\" mathsize=\"1.5em\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>p</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>q</mn></mrow></mfrac></math></p> <p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 1</b>: Representation of rational numbers.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "In this formula, both *p* and *q* are integers. By definition, this imposes a restriction on these numbers. As we studied in Volume 1 of *First Steps*, *q* cannot be zero, because it is impossible to share something with no one (<htcite0>).\n\nAnother fact that **Formula 1** shows is that there are more rational numbers than integers. To visualize this, let's recall the division of a sheet into two parts, presented in one of the *History Tracers* texts (<htcite1>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "3f68de7c-d729-4b62-9bb1-36c6f518fea4",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Simplicity vs. Complexity",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "09",
                        "day": "11"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "112e532d-e8af-40d6-b1b5-527978ee42f3",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Two sides",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table id=\"sequenceNum\" class=\"tableTen\"><tr><th colspan=\"2\">Divided sheet</th></tr><tr><td style=\"height: 300px;\"></td><td style=\"height: 300px;\"></td></tr> </table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 1</b>: Sheet divided into two pieces.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "From the sheet divided into 2, shown in **Table 1**, we can take 0 pieces, 1 piece, or 2 pieces. Each amount becomes a fraction whose denominator is 2 (the number of pieces the sheet was cut into) and whose numerator is the number of pieces taken. This gives the sequence:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math displaystyle=\"false\" mathsize=\"1.5em\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p> <p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 2</b>: Rational numbers with denominator 2.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 3: I am bigger than you</b><br /><br />Since rational numbers ℚ have more elements than natural numbers ℕ, how would you organize them on the sacred mountains of <b>Figure 1</b>?</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_irrational",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Irrational Numbers\n\nThese numbers have no dedicated symbol, and that is not the only curiosity they present.\n\nBy definition, irrational numbers are numbers that cannot be written using *Formula 1* with any pair of natural numbers *p* and *q*. Their digits after the decimal point — the decimals — never end and never repeat (<htcite0>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "2275f150-4769-4d20-8ac6-70b534e3c17c",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "Irrational Number",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2010",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 4: My Definition and My Nature</b><br /><br />The number pi (π) is an irrational number defined by dividing the circumference <b>C</b>—its perimeter—by the diameter <b>d</b> (π = C/d) (<htcite0>).<br /><br />Is this a contradiction?</div></div></center>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a56b7c6c-93ce-4e7f-bf5e-781e4597485d",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "If you cannot answer **Reflection 4**, do not worry — that is not a failure. It simply means there is more mathematics ahead. As you build more mathematical knowledge in *History Tracers*, you will be able to answer this reflection and visualize it.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_real",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Real Numbers (ℝ)\n\nReal numbers, represented by the symbol ℝ, are the numbers that encompass all the number sets studied so far (natural, rational, and irrational numbers) (<htcite0>), as well as others we will study later.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "fba167e6-42f1-4e7a-9dcd-677052cac50f",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 5: Is there still more?</b><br /><br />Before the previous paragraph, did you imagine that, in <i>History Tracers</i>, we had already covered all numbers?<br /><br />Does this change the way you were organizing the numbers in <b>Figure 1</b>?</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Continuity\n\nReal numbers are also called continuous numbers (<htcite0>) because they fill the “gaps” that exist between natural numbers. In **Graph 1** only whole-number inputs become points, so the points look separate. In **Graph 2** we multiply *every* value on the horizontal axis by 1 — not just the whole numbers — and those separate points join into one completely continuous line, with the natural numbers still marked as dots.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "fba167e6-42f1-4e7a-9dcd-677052cac50f",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htChart\"><canvas aria-label=\"chart\" id=\"chart4\" style=\"width:30em !important; height:20em !important;\"></canvas></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 2</b>: Result of multiplying all values on the horizontal axis by 1, with the natural numbers highlighted by dots. Graph created using the <htcite0> library.</div></p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "6efe7632-faed-4080-a5aa-38463d43e6d7",
                     "text": "Chart JS",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_drawing",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Drawing\n\nNow that the number groups have been presented — and that some are still missing — we can place the numbers we studied on the sacred mountains of **Figure 1**. We will explain each position right afterward.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p style=\"text-align:center;\"><div class=\"htSlidesGame\" style=\"display:block; width:max-content; max-width:100%; margin-left:auto; margin-right:auto;\"><img id=\"img1\" style=\"display:block; max-width:100%; max-height:80vh; margin:0 auto;\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('img1', '0%')\" class=\"imgGameSize\"/><div style=\"position: absolute; bottom: 46%; left: 52%; font-weight: bold; font-size:1.4em\">ℕ</div><div style=\"position: absolute; bottom: 67%; left: 70%; font-weight: bold; font-size:1.4em;\">ℚ</div><div style=\"position: absolute; bottom: 67%; left: 30%; font-weight: bold; font-size:1.4em; border-width: 3px solid black; border-radius: 25px; color: black;background-color: #4CAF50;\">&nbsp&nbsp;π&nbsp&nbsp;</div><div style=\"position: absolute; bottom: 90%; left: 55%; font-weight: bold; font-size:1.4em;\">ℝ</div></div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 3</b>: Number groups positioned within the sacred mountains of Copán.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Before analyzing the position of the number groups in **Figure 3**, note that Temple 16, in Copán, Honduras, is a continuous construction. From the first *ajaw* (king), around the year <htdate0>, to the last one, in the year <htdate1>, successive buildings were stacked one on top of the other (<htcite0>). Thanks to this superposition, we can associate the numbers we use every day with the different levels of the temple.\n\nFrom **Figure 3**, we can read the following approximations:\n- **Natural Numbers (ℕ)**: placed in the inner group, but not in the innermost one, because — as we saw — these numbers are infinite in quantity (<htcite1>). The point of this placement is that even the smallest group of numbers still contains many elements.\n- **The red region**: it represents the period of rulers 3 to 7 and has no number groups directly associated with it, because those numbers will be studied in other chapters.\n- **Rational Numbers (ℚ)**: in **Figure 3**, the yellow region that represents rational numbers looks isolated from the others, but it would not exist without the colored bases below it. In the same way, rational numbers are defined from the numbers placed below them (the integers).\n- **Irrational Numbers**: a green region has been added artificially at the same level as the rational numbers, and we placed the number pi (π) there to show that irrational numbers like it live at that level. Only one irrational number has been cited in *History Tracers* so far, but others will be studied later.\n- **Real Numbers (ℝ)**: finally, we placed the real numbers in the last part of the Temple 16 structure, showing that this group contains all the numbers already studied and **some** that we have not studied yet.",
